Title:
TrustMeBro — Authenticode Signature Manipulation Toolkit (SIP/FinalPolicy Hijack, PKCS#7 Embedding, SIPExec)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- TrustMeBro is a red-team/security-research toolkit focused on manipulating Windows Authenticode trust decisions and signed-content structures.  
- It supports signature stealing and metadata cloning, plus persistence and bypass techniques via SIP (Subject Interface Package) hijacking across 19 GUID/file-type handlers and WinVerifyTrust FinalPolicy trust-provider hijacking.  
- It also implements PKCS#7 payload embedding (“SigStash”) by hiding data inside signature structures (including a camouflage mode) while keeping signatures valid, and provides loaders/stubs to extract/execute embedded payloads.  
- The repo includes SIPExec, a lateral-movement/RCE chain that hijacks trust-provider registry settings and triggers WinVerifyTrust execution inside existing processes (notably WMI provider host), enabling shells over named pipes.  
- A Windows 11 Smart App Control (SAC) bypass is included by redirecting SAC SIP verification to a benign API path so unsigned binaries pass enforcement.  
- Useful for Red Teams/pentesters developing stealthy execution/persistence and for Blue Teams due to included Sigma/YARA detections and detailed SIP mapping/docs.

Technical Focus:
- Authenticode / WinVerifyTrust internals (SIP routing, trust providers, FinalPolicy)
- SIP registry hijacking across multiple file-type GUIDs (incl. WOW64-only option)
- PKCS#7 / ASN.1 DER manipulation (unsignedAttrs, nested signature camouflage)
- Smart App Control (Win11) enforcement bypass via SIP verification redirection
- Lateral movement via WMI + SMB staging and in-process execution (wmiprvse.exe)
- Analyst-triggered persistence via CryptDllFormatObject / OID handlers (FormatGhost)

Use Cases:
- Bypass or subvert Windows signature enforcement (SIP/FinalPolicy hijack) for execution of untrusted code
- Embed and later extract payloads from legitimately signed binaries while preserving signature validity
- Establish persistence by registering malicious SIP/OID handlers that load on verification/parsing events
- Remote registry-based deployment of trust-control hijacks using Impacket (or local via winreg)
- Lateral movement/RCE using SIPExec to execute inside WMI provider host without spawning a new process
- Defensive validation: hunt/detect SIP/FinalPolicy tampering using provided Sigma/YARA rules

Keywords:
Authenticode, WinVerifyTrust, SIP hijack, Subject Interface Package, Trust Provider, FinalPolicy, wintrust.dll, CryptSIPDllVerifyIndirectData, CryptSIPDllIsMyFileType2, PKCS#7, ASN.1 DER, unsignedAttrs, SPC_NESTED_SIGNATURE, CVE-2013-3900, Smart App Control, SrpCheckSmartlockerEAandProcessToken, DbgUiContinue, WMI, wmiprvse.exe, Impacket, Sigma, YARA, CryptDllFormatObject, OID handler, WOW64 registry, named pipe, SMB2